For the exercise, keep the shown ShelfKey definition and implement uniqueShelfCount(_:) for an array of keys. Set(keys) constructs a set from that array; .count returns its number of distinct entries. The original array is not edited. Here is a complete application:
```swift
struct ShelfKey: Hashable {
    let room: String
    let shelf: Int
}
func uniqueShelfCount(_ keys: [ShelfKey]) -> Int {
    return Set(keys).count
}
let key = ShelfKey(room: "west", shelf: 7)
print(uniqueShelfCount([key, key]))
```
The final line prints 1. Optional local practice: predict the count for an empty array and for a third room with the same shelf number.

after:
```swift
struct ShelfKey: Hashable {
    let room: String
    let shelf: Int
}
let a = ShelfKey(room: "east", shelf: 2)
let same = ShelfKey(room: "east", shelf: 2)
let different = ShelfKey(room: "east", shelf: 3)
let keys: Set<ShelfKey> = [a, same, different]
print(a == same)
print(a == different)
print(keys.count)
print(keys.contains(same))
```
This prints true, false, 2, and true. a and same match, so they take one place in the set. different has another shelf number, so it takes another place.
